Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 7/31/2026

FADE IN:

EXT. OLD MAN'S HOUSE - NIGHT

A narrow, weathered house stands beneath a moonless sky. Its shuttered windows are black.

(SOUND: wind scrapes dry branches across the shutters)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

An OLD MAN, thin and silver-haired in a white nightshirt, sleeps beneath a heavy dark quilt. A brass watch rests beside his bed.

(SOUND: a clock ticks behind the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this!

A dark lantern remains closed in the crack of the bedroom door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

The OLD MAN shifts in sleep. One eyelid flutters but stays closed.

(SOUND: bedsprings give a faint creak)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so I made up my mind to take the life of the old man.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Thus I would rid myself of the eye forever.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A week passes in brief repetitions: the door latch turns; a pale hand inches in the closed lantern; a dark-clad NARRATOR leans through the opening.

(SOUND: the door latch clicks almost silently)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You should have seen how wisely I proceeded—with what caution—with what foresight—with what dissimulation I went to work!

The NARRATOR, lean and pale, with dark hair combed flat and a plain black waistcoat, opens the door a fraction farther.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

A thin ray slips from the lantern and lands on the OLD MAN'S shut eyelid.

(SOUND: lantern hinges creak softly)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - MORNING

Gray daylight fills the room. The OLD MAN, silver-haired and stooped in a brown dressing gown, sits upright in bed.

(SOUND: sparrows chirp beyond the closed shutters)

The NARRATOR enters openly, smiling too broadly, his black waistcoat buttoned neatly.

NARRATOR
How have you passed the night?

The OLD MAN watches him with one pale blue, filmed eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

On the eighth night, the NARRATOR eases open the door with agonizing slowness. The room is black as pitch behind its fastened shutters.

(SOUND: a watch ticks in the darkness)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A watch’s minute hand moves more quickly than did mine.

The NARRATOR bites back a chuckle. The OLD MAN jolts awake in bed.

(SOUND: bedsprings snap under the OLD MAN)

OLD MAN
Who’s there?

The NARRATOR freezes behind the door, lantern clutched to his chest.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a whole hour I did not move a muscle.

The OLD MAN sits rigidly upright, facing the darkness.

(SOUND: a deathwatch beetle taps within the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was still sitting up in the bed listening; just as I have done, night after night, hearkening to the death watches in the wall.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The OLD MAN'S shoulders curl inward. A low groan escapes him as he stares toward the invisible doorway.

(SOUND: the OLD MAN releases a stifled groan)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the groan of mortal terror. It was not a groan of pain or of grief.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the low stifled sound that arises from the bottom of the soul when overcharged with awe.

The NARRATOR'S eye glints in the narrow darkness beyond the door.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had been saying to himself: it is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor.

A faint crevice opens in the lantern.

(SOUND: the lantern's tin fastening scrapes under a thumb)

A spider-thin beam crosses the bed and strikes the OLD MAN'S open eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The pale blue eye fills the ray of light, its cloudy film stark against the dark.

(SOUND: a low, quick heartbeat begins beneath the room's silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man’s heart.

The NARRATOR holds the lantern perfectly still. Sweat beads on his pale face.

(SOUND: the heartbeat grows quicker and louder)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The hellish tattoo of the heart increased. It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I thought the heart must burst. A new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

The NARRATOR throws open the lantern and lunges into the room.

(SOUND: the lantern snaps open; the OLD MAN shrieks once)

The OLD MAN topples from the bed. The NARRATOR drags him to the floor and heaves the heavy bedstead over him.

(SOUND: the bed crashes down; the heartbeat muffles beneath it)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The old man’s hour had come!

The NARRATOR kneels beside the bed, listening without blinking.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound.

The heartbeat stops. The NARRATOR removes the bed and presses a hand to the OLD MAN'S chest.

(SOUND: silence, broken only by the ticking clock)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. He was stone, stone dead.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The NARRATOR, sleeves rolled above his wrists, works on the floor beside a wooden tub. The OLD MAN'S bed stands stripped and pushed aside.

(SOUND: a saw bites through wood; the old clock ticks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The night waned, and I worked hastily, but in silence.

The NARRATOR lifts three floor planks. He lowers wrapped remains between the scantlings, then fits the boards back into place.

(SOUND: floorboards settle into their grooves)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected anything wrong.

The room is orderly again. The NARRATOR wipes his hands on the inside of his black waistcoat.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever.

INT. HOUSE ENTRY HALL - PRE-DAWN

Four o'clock darkness presses against the glass of the street door. The NARRATOR descends the stairs, composed in his black waistcoat.

(SOUND: a clock bell strikes four)

A sharp knock sounds at the door.

(SOUND: three firm knocks against the street door)

The NARRATOR opens it to three POLICE OFFICERS in dark coats and brimmed hats. They silently show their badges.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A shriek had been heard by a neighbour during the night. Suspicion of foul play had been aroused.

The NARRATOR steps aside with an inviting smile.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I bade the gentlemen welcome. The shriek, I said, was my own in a dream.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The old man, I mentioned, was absent in the country.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The three POLICE OFFICERS inspect the tidy room. The OLD MAN'S treasures remain secure in an open chest.

(SOUND: boot heels cross the wooden floor)

The NARRATOR leads them to the center of the room, still pale and neatly dressed in his black waistcoat.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I bade them search—search well.

The officers complete their inspection and gather chairs. The NARRATOR brings a fourth chair and places it directly over the hidden floorboards.

(SOUND: chair legs scrape across the planks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the wild audacity of my perfect triumph, I placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse.

The NARRATOR sits. The officers relax and converse quietly among themselves, their words indistinct.

(SOUND: low, pleasant police chatter)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The NARRATOR'S smile fades. His fingers tighten around the chair arms while the officers continue their quiet talk.

(SOUND: a faint ringing grows beneath the officers' chatter)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone.

The NARRATOR rubs his temple, then speaks rapidly to the officers, forcing a grin.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I talked more freely to get rid of the feeling; but it continued and gained definiteness.

The sound resolves into the low, dull, quick beat of a heart.

(SOUND: a muffled heartbeat pulses beneath the floorboards)

The NARRATOR rises and paces with heavy strides. The officers smile, apparently untroubled.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I found that the noise was not within my ears.

The heartbeat swells. The NARRATOR swings his chair up and grates its legs violently across the floor.

(SOUND: chair legs grind over wood; the heartbeat grows louder)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone?

The officers keep talking pleasantly. The NARRATOR whirls on them, face drained white, dark hair disordered.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

The heartbeat pounds over every other sound.

(SOUND: the heartbeat hammers, deafening and relentless)

NARRATOR
Villains! dissemble no more!

The NARRATOR points wildly at the floorboards beneath his chair.

NARRATOR
I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!

NARRATOR
It is the beating of his hideous heart!

FADE OUT.
